WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.680
ѐѐѐ spoѐѐѐ ѐѐѐ ѐѐ ѐѐ ѐѐ ѐѐѐѐ ѐѐѐѐ ѐѐѐѐѐ ѐѐѐ ѐѐѐѐ ѐѐ ѐѐѐѐ ѐѐѐѐ ѐѐ,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:41.920
Over one third of our planet is frozen, and yet the icy worlds of the Arctic and Antarctic

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:48.880
are as alien to most of us as the surface of another planet.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.920
They are places of superlatives, from ice caps that hold nearly 80% of our planet's

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:04.800
fresh water to frozen forests that encircle the entire globe.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.880
These are places that feed our imaginations, places that seem to be borrowed from fairy

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:10.880
tales.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:24.800
They are dominated and shaped by the ice, both by its coming and by its going.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.200
This is our planet's last true wilderness, and one that is changing just as we're beginning

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.240
to understand it.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:40.120
In this series, we'll be travelling to all parts of these lonely lands, both north and

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:46.920
south, to witness its wonders, perhaps for the last time, and to discover some extraordinary

00:01:46.920 --> 00:02:16.760
examples of survival against all the odds as can be found anywhere on the planet.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.480
The poles are permanently capped with ice.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:29.920
Nowhere is colder, windier or more hostile to life.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.880
I'm standing at the North Pole, the very top of the Earth.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.520
Up here, it's easy to see why the polar regions are so cold.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:49.360
The sun never rises high enough in the sky to warm my back, and those rays that do strike

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.840
the surface are mostly reflected back from this great whiteness.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:04.200
But the fundamental problem is that there's no sun here at all for half the year.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:12.360
The polar winter is unrivaled in its harshness, a night that lasts for months.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:18.680
Only the toughest day, as temperatures plunge to minus 70 degrees centigrade.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:24.920
And yet, the greatest challenge to life here is not the cold, but the extreme swings between

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.600
the seasons.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:35.080
When the sun finally returns, an extraordinary transformation begins.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:43.320
This frozen world begins to melt away.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:50.620
The polar spring brings a brief opportunity for life.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:04:00.720
By summer, the sun no longer sets and works its magic for 24 hours a day.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.480
Now it's a race to breed before the sun departs.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:22.080
By autumn, all but the hardiest abandon the poles and the ice extends its grip.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:38.320
Land and sea close down for the long polar winter until once again the sun returns.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.360
It's spring in the high Arctic, and the sun illuminates a giant frozen ocean, the first

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:50.280
stop on our journey.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:05:00.400
The most powerful land predator is on the prowl.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:07.440
A male polar bear is searching for a mate.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.360
Willing females are few and far between, and the sea ice on which he travels will soon

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.360
melt and vanish.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:38.200
He's running out of time to find a mate in this vast frozen desert.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.920
Ten miles ahead, a single female without cubs.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.680
Can you see what the male is seeking?

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:54.600
He seems to relish her scent even though she's miles away.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.360
This is an exciting prospect.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.200
She's clearly giving off the right signals.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.580
He locks onto her tracks, eager not to lose her trail.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:14.360
It's easier to tread in the compacted snow of her footprints.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:23.440
This pursuit could last for days.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.520
The female eventually comes into view.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:43.360
The search is finally over.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:49.080
For the female, only half his weight, this must be a nerve-wracking encounter.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.920
The male could kill her if he chooses.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:59.360
But he has other intentions.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.740
And she is ready and willing.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:13.160
She leads him to higher ground.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:18.880
It seems that courting polar bears prefer privacy, often leaving the sea ice and heading

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:28.720
for the hills to avoid the prying eyes of rival males who might disturb them.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:31.280
Few have witnessed this moment.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:42.760
For the male, his only tender encounter in an otherwise solitary life.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.480
But it doesn't last long.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.760
A rival suitor has also caught the female's scent.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.400
Courtship has to be put on hold.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:08:05.460
He must fight for his rights.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:10.640
He sees off this first challenger without injury to either party.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:26.280
Great bloodier battles are to come.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:45.520
Another battle won, though he has been slightly injured.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:52.880
He hurries back to his mate, but now she seems to have lost her enthusiasm.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:09:11.120
Male polar bears are high maintenance.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:26.520
Wherever she goes, he will follow, mating with her when she allows and guarding her

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.800
at all times.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:36.200
Over the next two weeks, the male sees off many rivals, but the battles take their toll

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:43.080
on him.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:54.560
He is almost spent, but he has ensured that no other bears have mated with his female.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.560
It's time for the couple to go their separate ways.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:08.480
She will give birth to his cubs alone in nine months' time, and he may never see her again.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:15.000
He returns to the frozen ocean, no doubt relieved to resume his solitary ways.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:23.800
And just in time, the ice beneath his feet will soon be gone.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:29.840
That spring, the Arctic Ocean undergoes an extraordinary transformation.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:38.760
An area of sea ice the size of Europe melts, exposing the rich waters beneath.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:51.400
Short-tailed shearwaters have travelled 10,000 miles from Australia to be here.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:11:13.640
18 million visitors darken the skies, the largest gathering of seabirds on the planet.

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:23.600
Humpback whales have come all the way from the equator to feed in these rich polar waters.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:33.320
Their giant tails are five metres across.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:38.120
Simply raising them above the surface gives the whales enough downward momentum to reach

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:56.440
the great swarms of krill and herring below.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:19.680
The shearwaters follow the giant's lead.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:26.440
For those who can get here in summer, these waters provide a feast of epic proportions.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:35.920
But the good times will be very short, a problem that faces all life in the polar regions.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:41.360
Journeying south across the Arctic Ocean, the first land you reach is Greenland, the

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:45.200
largest island in the world.

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:51.680
Despite its name, Greenland is mostly white, covered by a jar die sheet six times the size

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:57.640
of the United Kingdom.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:01.520
In the middle of the island, the ice is nearly two miles thick.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:10.080
It's a bleak, quiet world.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:20.440
Sapphire blue melt lakes are the first sign that a dynamic process is underway.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:29.600
Each lake forms in a matter of days, expanding until it's miles across and starts to overflow.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:45.160
The spill water then carves its way through the ice.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:50.560
The water courses through an icy delta like blood along the arteries of a cold-blooded

00:13:50.560 --> 00:14:07.000
monster, a monster that is stirring.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:13.920
And without warning, the water suddenly plunges down an open shaft, falling a vertical mile

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:40.440
into the heart of the ice sheet.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:44.200
This melt water has a surprising effect.

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:50.080
It lubricates the junction between the ice and the rock floor beneath, so the entire ice

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:58.320
sheet is now on the move, sliding downhill into the ocean.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:05.560
This, Jakobshaven Isbre, is the fastest-flowing glacier on our planet, moving as much as 40

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:10.160
meters a day.

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:16.400
As it advances, it destroys everything in its path, even cutting its way through Greenland's

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:24.520
great mountain ranges on its drive downwards towards the sea.

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:36.380
When speeded up, these solid rivers of ice seem to flow just like liquid rivers.

00:15:36.380 --> 00:16:00.980
This is the titanic force that cuts down mountains and levels the surface of continents.

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:07.840
The ice is now entering the last stage of its descent.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:19.080
As it gains speed, huge crevasses open that extend down to its very core.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:24.000
It's reached the ocean, and millions of tons of ice have lost the support of their rocky

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:29.200
bed.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:51.320
Everything must give.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:56.120
These ice falls are an ominous sign of what is about to happen.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:59.160
A rupture deep within the glacier.

00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:54.440
A colossal iceberg is born.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:59.840
This single block of ice, many hundreds of meters across, would dwarf the biggest of

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:09.160
mankind's buildings.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:14.960
Every year, tens of thousands of icebergs are spawned by Greenland's glaciers, and their

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:30.480
number is steadily increasing as the climate continues the warm.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:38.200
The breakup of the bergs fills the bays of the Arctic with exquisite ice sculptures.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:45.320
It also releases great volumes of cold, fresh water into the sea.

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:50.480
Greenland's meltwater influences the course of the ocean currents, which in turn has an

00:18:50.480 --> 00:19:02.280
effect on the weather around the world.

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:06.400
The Arctic is closer to home than many of us realize.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:11.080
It includes the northernmost parts of the three continents on which most of us live

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:17.560
– Europe, Asia, and North America.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:23.640
The first bare land we reach on our journey south is a bleak, treeless wilderness known

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:27.600
as Tundra.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:33.040
Each spring, animals travel up from the south to be ready for the rich grazing that will

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:36.920
be unveiled by the spring melt.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:39.160
For the caribou, the timing is critical.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:42.360
Arrive early and a winter storm could kill you.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:57.240
Delay too long and you may fail to lay down the fat needed to survive a polar winter.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:07.800
Both the south still and stooped shrouded figures end the flat monotony of the tundra.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:12.900
This is the tree line, the first place on our journey with sufficient warmth and liquid

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:18.400
water to enable a tree to grow.

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:23.320
Surviving here is so crushingly difficult that it can take hundreds of years for a seedling

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:34.640
to grow into a stunted shrub.

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:40.560
But even small trees can provide cover for a predator.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.360
Wolves.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:47.000
These in northern Canada are the largest and most powerful in the world and they're setting

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:52.800
out to hunt.

00:20:52.800 --> 00:21:07.200
The pack is 25 strong, a sign that the prey they're seeking is formidable.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:12.120
These bison are even bigger than their southern cousins and the largest land animals in North

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.240
America.

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:20.000
For generations, wolves and bison here have been shaped by their battles with each other,

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:25.360
making each the most impressive of its kind.

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:28.680
The bison will not stay long among the trees.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:36.760
They're not safe here.

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:42.400
The wolves are closing in, but their chance of ambushing the bison in the woods has passed.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:49.600
Their prey are now in the open and grouped together for safety.

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:57.480
The wolves will need to work as a team if they're to make a kill.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:01.620
They circle the herd, try to unsettle it and split it up.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:03.920
But the bison are armed and dangerous.

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:12.800
They will be safe as long as they stick together.

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:17.920
The wolves up their game, harrying the herd, a ploy to trigger a stampede and split away

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:22.440
one of the small ones.

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:33.840
The bison form a defensive circle around their young, horns pointing outwards.

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:42.720
The wolves need a bison to break rank.

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:49.960
But the tables are turning and now the wolves have to retreat.

00:22:49.960 --> 00:23:05.360
The pack focus their attention on the rear of the herd and the bison begin to panic.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:30.040
A

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:38.560
young bison falls behind.

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:49.240
Even this yearling dwarfs the wolves.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:54.040
Running head down, the herd's only thought is escape.

00:23:54.040 --> 00:24:13.520
A stroke of luck for the wolves.

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:20.600
The kill will feed the pack for several days, but then they will have to resume the chase.

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:36.560
At the frozen ends of our planet, the struggle for survival never eases.

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:43.440
South of the tree line, the winters are shorter so trees grow faster and taller and forests

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:46.520
begin to appear.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:51.920
As the warm, humid air from the south meets the cold arctic air, the moisture it carries

00:24:51.920 --> 00:25:00.240
crystallizes and snowflakes fall from the sky.

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:09.640
Each crystal forms around a particle of dust.

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:24.040
All have a six-fold symmetry, but no two have ever been found with exactly the same shape.

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:33.400
Their variety and complexity is breathtaking.

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:38.080
Each snowflake is water waiting to be released in spring.

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:44.280
For this reason, snow is the lifeblood of these silent forests, and all that live here

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:53.800
depend on it in one way or another.

00:25:53.800 --> 00:26:03.800
Some like the great gray owl appear in spring for the boom times, then vanish like phantoms.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:10.920
Others, like their lemming prey, are here year-round beneath the snow, insulated from

00:26:10.920 --> 00:26:33.760
the cold air above.

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:39.560
The northern forests are a crossroads for seasonal visitors and arctic specialists.

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:43.200
But they are so much more than this.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:49.600
Together, they make up the taiga, an unbroken belt of forest that stretches 7,000 miles

00:26:49.600 --> 00:27:05.200
around our planet and contains one-third of all the trees on Earth.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:10.760
The taiga forest marks the end of our journey through the arctic, from the frozen ocean

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:22.200
down across the lands that surround it.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:29.720
The other end of our planet, the Antarctic, is starkly different, a frozen continent completely

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:34.280
surrounded by ocean.

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:38.920
Icebergs here are so large that they're measured in miles, not meters.

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:44.680
They're the only obstacles in the path of giant waves which circle around the continent

00:27:44.680 --> 00:28:01.880
unchecked by other lands.

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:28.640
These seas may be cold and storm-wracked, but they're bursting with life.

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:47.800
No bird is more at home in water, and they are masterful surfers.

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:52.600
Penguins are found only in the southern hemisphere.

00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:55.680
They can't fly, but they don't need to.

00:28:55.680 --> 00:29:07.080
There are no polar bears here.

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:10.000
These are gentoo penguins.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:14.360
Each spring they come ashore to lay their eggs and rear their young.

00:29:14.360 --> 00:29:23.080
Their hungry chicks demand so much seafood that both parents have to go fishing.

00:29:23.080 --> 00:29:43.280
And fishing can be dangerous.

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:50.640
A southern sea lion.

00:29:50.640 --> 00:30:10.200
It uses the speed of a breaking wave to catch up with the gentoos.

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:15.240
Sea lions normally eat fish, so he's used to catching streamlined swimmers.

00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:19.160
But the gentoo seem more than his match out at sea.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:25.920
He must change tactics.

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:50.840
Perhaps it will be easier in the shallows.

00:30:50.840 --> 00:31:00.840
No, it seems penguins are uncatchable in water.

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:04.840
How about on land?

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:10.080
The penguins' wings, so powerful for swimming, are of no help when it comes to running.

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:17.960
Now surely the sea lion has a chance.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:44.160
And on the beach, both are like fish out of water.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:48.680
Rarely do hunter and hunted play their roles with so little skill.

00:31:48.680 --> 00:32:15.120
The outcome is anyone's guess.

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:35.720
Every summer, over 40 million penguins take to the southern ocean to feed.

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:41.640
They're joined by thousands of whales.

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:46.160
The pinkies are the most numerous.

00:32:46.160 --> 00:32:52.880
They all come here to harvest the richest ocean on earth.

00:32:52.880 --> 00:33:13.000
Carrying on south, we get our first glimpse of the frozen continent.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:23.080
Even humpbacks, after traveling 4,000 miles from the equator, are finally arriving in

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:25.600
Antarctica.

00:33:25.600 --> 00:33:29.040
Humans have long felt the lure of this mysterious world.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:35.480
Yet it was only 100 years ago that the first explorers walked inland and were confronted

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:45.320
by the highest, driest and coldest territory on earth.

00:33:45.320 --> 00:34:00.760
Every year, the continent is transformed as the sea ice that surrounds it begins to disappear.

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:06.160
This melt halves the size of Antarctica.

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:15.880
It's the most spectacular seasonal change occurring anywhere on our planet.

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:25.200
The remnants of the sea ice are occupied by sunbathing seals that have been here all winter.

00:34:25.200 --> 00:34:39.000
But new arrivals are following the retreating ice edge, and they have come here to hunt.

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:44.040
Killer whales, the ocean's top predator.

00:34:44.040 --> 00:34:48.760
Killers are like wolves, for they will hunt animals far larger than themselves.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:58.120
But even smaller prey are a problem if you can't reach them.

00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:03.200
The solution is teamwork.

00:35:03.200 --> 00:35:08.600
Swimming in perfect formation, they flick their tails in unison and create a wave that

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:18.040
cracks the ice.

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:21.320
They regroup and assess the damage.

00:35:21.320 --> 00:35:28.840
A more powerful wave is needed.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:33.160
The ice flow is breaking up.

00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:36.800
Now they are close enough to get a good look at their target.

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:41.240
The seal is a crab eater, sharp-toothed and feisty.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:46.400
Not their favorite.

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:52.240
The wolves of the sea move on in search of easier quarry.

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:59.080
A whittle seal, that's better.

00:35:59.080 --> 00:36:04.600
These are more docile and easier to tackle.

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:11.120
The pod stays close together and travels silently.

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:24.000
This time they unleash a far more powerful wave, and with astonishing accuracy.

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:29.520
These big waves are not intended to break the ice, but to knock the prey into the water,

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:52.160
something rarely failed.

00:36:52.160 --> 00:37:19.560
The seal is now where the killers wanted.

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:23.960
But the hunt is far from over.

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:30.840
They need to grab their prey by the tail while avoiding its snapping jaws.

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:38.320
Only then will they be able to pull it down and drown it.

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:50.480
Side swipes create violent underwater turbulence, a new tactic.

00:37:50.480 --> 00:38:02.960
Blowing bubbles gives cover for others to lunge at the seal's tail.

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:11.720
Somehow the seal manages to reach a tiny ice flow.

00:38:11.720 --> 00:38:19.080
The killers could easily grab it, but now this seems to become a game.

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:34.800
The seal's life hangs on a roll of the ice.

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:56.480
Yet again the pod joins forces to dislodge the seal.

00:38:56.480 --> 00:39:03.160
The seal sees a chance to escape.

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:09.920
Exhausted it no longer has the energy to pull itself to safety, and the killers are moving

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:19.600
in.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:32.960
Game over.

00:39:32.960 --> 00:39:38.680
Although such team hunts are rarely seen, scientists believe they may be the most complex

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:41.680
ever documented in the natural world.

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:46.680
They were first witnessed by Captain Scott and his men when they came to explore Antarctica

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:52.240
100 years ago.

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:58.080
Journeying further south, the fragmenting ice is replaced by a permanent sheet that

00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:02.480
doesn't melt even at the height of summer.

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:08.360
It's a barrier that many creatures find impossible.

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:11.640
It repels even powerful minke whales.

00:40:11.640 --> 00:40:23.520
They have to turn back if they can no longer reach the air they need to breathe.

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:31.120
Under the ice, life has to be extremely specialized to survive.

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:38.360
Few of us will ever experience this strangely still world, and as yet no one knows much

00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:41.360
about it.

00:40:41.360 --> 00:40:50.120
The crystalline surface of the ice stalactites provides a home for ice fish whose bodies

00:40:50.120 --> 00:40:57.080
are full of antifreeze.

00:40:57.080 --> 00:41:02.760
The ceiling of ice shields those living below it from the violent polar weather that rages

00:41:02.760 --> 00:41:03.760
above.

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:10.720
Little here has changed for millions of years.

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:31.680
The cold allows animals to grow very slowly and become giants.

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:38.520
The relative of the woodlouse is the size of a dinner plate.

00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:46.920
And this so-called sea spider has legs that span half a meter.

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:56.240
Now explorers are revealing other worlds that lie hidden beneath the ice on land.

00:41:56.240 --> 00:42:04.000
These smoking towers are the gateway to a network of caves.

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:17.960
Each contains an extraordinary assembly of ice crystals unlike any other on earth.

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:24.080
Like snowflakes, every crystal is unique.

00:42:24.080 --> 00:42:29.240
Some are taller than a man.

00:42:29.240 --> 00:42:35.040
These are thought to harbor life, seeded by strange bacteria that thrive in these extreme

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:36.040
conditions.

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:43.160
The breeze that gently sways these crystals is responsible for making them.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:49.000
It's steam from the molten heart of Mount Erebus, the most southerly volcano on our

00:42:49.000 --> 00:43:01.880
planet.

00:43:01.880 --> 00:43:07.400
It's now thought that the ice caves fringing this crater may even be a home for hitherto

00:43:07.400 --> 00:43:14.700
unknown life forms.

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:20.120
From this oasis of warmth at the edge of the continent, our journey continues inland towards

00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:22.640
the South Pole.

00:43:22.640 --> 00:43:30.360
The first great hurdle is the formidable Transantarctic mountain range.

00:43:30.360 --> 00:43:35.120
We are following the route taken by Scott and Amundsen as they struggle to become the

00:43:35.120 --> 00:43:43.080
first humans to reach the South Pole.

00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:48.080
They were traveling on foot, and their first sight of these mountains must have been daunting

00:43:48.080 --> 00:43:49.760
indeed.

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:55.240
In front of them stretched one of the world's longest ranges, spanning 2,000 miles from

00:43:55.240 --> 00:44:01.400
one side of the continent to the other.

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:04.080
The winds up here are the fastest on earth.

00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:10.560
They reach speeds of 200 miles an hour.

00:44:10.560 --> 00:44:16.600
An ice-capped mountain bears the scars of the gales, bizarre sculptures carved from

00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:29.240
solid ice.

00:44:29.240 --> 00:44:33.280
It's not only the ice that yields.

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:39.560
This sculptured spire is the remnant of a mountain eroded from all sides by the ferocious

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:44.160
elements.

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:56.280
Beyond a wholly unexpected landscape, the dry valleys.

00:44:56.280 --> 00:45:10.000
Only one percent of Antarctica is free of ice, and most of that bare rock is here.

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:19.880
The dry valleys are more like the surface of Mars than is any other place on earth.

00:45:19.880 --> 00:45:29.880
The floor is covered with extraordinary natural sculptures, created by the same winds that

00:45:29.880 --> 00:45:34.280
help to keep these valleys free of snow.

00:45:34.280 --> 00:45:47.700
Over time, entire boulders are weathered from the inside out until just a shell remains.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:55.080
At the head of these valleys, ice is making a breakthrough.

00:45:55.080 --> 00:46:11.080
Millions of tons are tumbling in slow motion into the valley.

00:46:11.080 --> 00:46:21.840
These ice blocks are the size of skyscrapers.

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:32.640
And this is the Beardmoor glacier, which Scott and his men somehow traversed on foot.

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:43.320
It's over 100 miles long and one of the largest glaciers on earth.

00:46:43.320 --> 00:46:55.840
But nothing could have prepared those early explorers for what they were about to encounter.

00:46:55.840 --> 00:47:02.440
The Antarctic Ice Cap, the largest expanse of ice on the planet.

00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:12.160
It's three miles thick in places and imprisons 70 percent of the world's fresh water.

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:30.360
From here to the South Pole, 700 miles away, there is nothing but ice.

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:34.920
I'm at the South Pole at the end of my journey.

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:43.240
Although it's midsummer, the temperature here is a bone chilling 35 degrees below freezing.

00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:50.760
It's exactly 100 years, almost the day that the first human being stood right here.

00:47:50.760 --> 00:47:54.280
Amundsen followed by Scott.

00:47:54.280 --> 00:48:02.240
In those days, reaching the poles was regarded as the ultimate in human endeavor and endurance

00:48:02.240 --> 00:48:05.480
and a source of great national pride.

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:13.600
Today, the polar regions have a rather different significance because now we've come to understand

00:48:13.600 --> 00:48:21.400
that what happens here and in the North affects every one of us, no matter where we live on

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:28.400
this planet.

00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:56.620
The greatest challenge for the team making frozen planet was the extreme remoteness of

00:48:56.620 --> 00:48:58.680
their locations.

00:48:58.680 --> 00:49:07.020
Many of the shoots lasted months at a time and needed a number of crews to join forces.

00:49:07.020 --> 00:49:12.200
One location that would require such siege tactics was Mount Erebus, Antarctica's most

00:49:12.200 --> 00:49:14.960
active volcano.

00:49:14.960 --> 00:49:21.960
This magical mountain does not give up her secrets easily.

00:49:24.840 --> 00:49:29.560
To capture the full story of Mount Erebus from top to bottom required four different

00:49:29.560 --> 00:49:33.720
film crews.

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:40.720
The cave team has dropped off at 12,000 feet, close to the crater.

00:49:40.720 --> 00:49:47.720
In howling winds and thin oxygen, their challenge is to find a way into the volcano itself.

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:53.440
They are venturing into the unknown.

00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:59.080
Somewhere below are spectacular ice caves melted out by volcanic steam.

00:49:59.080 --> 00:50:03.560
Getting the team safely underground is a relief for director Chad and Hunter.

00:50:03.560 --> 00:50:05.720
Excellent, it's a lot warmer down here.

00:50:05.720 --> 00:50:08.760
It's freezing up there, minus 29.

00:50:08.760 --> 00:50:11.440
With him is cameraman Gavin Thurston.

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:13.640
You sort of forget being in here.

00:50:13.640 --> 00:50:16.680
You are actually inside a volcano.

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:20.240
You know, above us, there's, and below us, there's bubbling lava.

00:50:20.240 --> 00:50:23.760
And you've got all these gases seeping up through here, which is how these caves are

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:24.280
made.

00:50:24.280 --> 00:50:27.760
So there's also increased carbon dioxide in here.

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:32.480
As the cave team head deeper, dangerous volcanic gases made breathing difficult.

00:50:32.480 --> 00:50:34.960
The clock is ticking.

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:40.920
They will not have long to find the caves of crystals.

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:46.640
Above ground, the aerial team is pushing for the summit of Erebus.

00:50:46.640 --> 00:50:51.520
Series producer Vanessa Berlewits directs from the front seat, while aerial cameraman

00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:54.800
Michael Kellum controls the camera attached to the nose.

00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:56.800
We're going to be around 14,000 feet.

00:50:56.800 --> 00:50:59.800
We're going to be at the performance limits of this aircraft.

00:50:59.800 --> 00:51:03.800
Any bad weather comes in up there, you're pretty much screwed really.

00:51:03.800 --> 00:51:06.520
You've got to get up the mountain fast.

00:51:06.520 --> 00:51:13.360
Above 10,000 feet, the pilot must breathe oxygen through a plastic tube in his nostrils.

00:51:13.360 --> 00:51:16.400
Approaching the crater, conditions do not look good.

00:51:16.400 --> 00:51:22.520
Today, Erebus is belching out steam and gases, making flying extremely risky.

00:51:22.520 --> 00:51:28.880
Up here, the air is so thin, the helicopter can't hover and must keep moving.

00:51:28.880 --> 00:51:33.440
This is aerial filming at its most extreme.

00:51:33.440 --> 00:51:35.440
They struggle to get a clear view.

00:51:35.440 --> 00:51:40.440
We're just coming up to 14,000 feet.

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:45.440
You can actually look right into the lava lake.

00:51:45.440 --> 00:51:48.440
That's looking really good.

00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:58.440
The cameraman captures a rare shot of the molten lava, but it's soon obscured again.

00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:00.200
The volcano is temperamental.

00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:06.000
The team have seized a rare opportunity to see into its molten heart, but now they must

00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:09.880
descend to safety.

00:52:09.880 --> 00:52:21.760
As the weather closes in above, the cave team are making progress below.

00:52:21.760 --> 00:52:28.600
It's Christmas Day and the crew are dressed for the occasion.

00:52:28.600 --> 00:52:35.760
Just watch my back on these icicles, I don't want to snap that top one off.

00:52:35.760 --> 00:52:40.880
How strong are these pillars of ice, Matt?

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:43.880
It's about that round, about five.

00:52:43.880 --> 00:52:47.520
So if I squeeze past, it's not going to snap it.

00:52:47.520 --> 00:52:52.480
Gavin is reassured by advice from the scientist.

00:52:52.480 --> 00:52:58.880
Oh, Gavin!

00:52:58.880 --> 00:53:02.160
I'm sorry.

00:53:02.160 --> 00:53:03.160
It's terrible.

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:09.840
Oh, look, it fits perfectly, look at that.

00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:14.800
Fortunately these crystals are made of frozen water and can grow back in weeks.

00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:20.160
We've got these beautiful clear glass-like pillars and right next to it, this really

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:26.600
delicate, look how thin that filament is there.

00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:32.640
As the cave team explore deeper, each chamber reveals ice crystals more strange and spectacular

00:53:32.640 --> 00:53:38.080
than the last.

00:53:38.080 --> 00:53:47.880
No one on the team imagined a single Antarctic mountain could house so many wonders.

00:53:47.880 --> 00:53:53.280
Down at the foot of Mount Erebus, a third crew, the dive team, plan to explore the volcano's

00:53:53.280 --> 00:53:58.120
lower slopes which extend beneath the frozen sea.

00:53:58.120 --> 00:54:00.280
Onboard is underwater cameraman Hugh Miller.

00:54:00.280 --> 00:54:06.640
The problem is we don't actually know what's under the ice here, so who knows, it's a bit

00:54:06.640 --> 00:54:08.320
of an adventure.

00:54:08.320 --> 00:54:10.160
Old-fashioned tools still work best.

00:54:10.160 --> 00:54:17.760
First, a hand chisel to create an opening, then a saw to widen the hole.

00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:22.160
Ice diving in the coldest waters on the planet should be taken extremely seriously.

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:25.560
This dive's going to be a lot of things.

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:29.040
Warm is not on that list.

00:54:29.040 --> 00:54:34.600
Insulated suits will keep them alive under the ice for only 60 minutes.

00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:42.360
Once the helicopter departs, there's no margin for error.

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:47.840
The dive team begin to explore the lower slopes of Erebus, discovering a hidden world rarely

00:54:47.840 --> 00:54:52.120
seen by humans.

00:54:52.120 --> 00:54:59.160
Patrolling the icy shores of the volcano are killer whales, the most southerly in the world.

00:54:59.160 --> 00:55:05.200
Trekking them from above is the orca team.

00:55:05.200 --> 00:55:09.440
They need a helicopter to get ahead of the whales and to land them on the fragile sea

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:13.080
ice.

00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:16.240
Cameraman Jamie MacPherson must pick his spot carefully.

00:55:16.240 --> 00:55:21.480
His aim is to get the cameras as close to the killer whales as possible without disturbing

00:55:21.480 --> 00:55:22.480
them.

00:55:22.480 --> 00:55:36.680
He uses a film camera to capture the action in slow motion.

00:55:36.680 --> 00:55:41.520
And orcas come right by him.

00:55:41.520 --> 00:55:47.440
Even in the extreme cold, a film camera proves to be rugged and reliable, provided there's

00:55:47.440 --> 00:55:49.440
enough film in the camera.

00:55:49.440 --> 00:55:52.440
End of the rock.

00:55:52.440 --> 00:55:53.440
No!

00:55:53.440 --> 00:55:56.440
I got him coming out.

00:55:56.440 --> 00:55:58.920
I just didn't get him going back in.

00:55:58.920 --> 00:56:12.640
Below the sea ice, the dive team is setting up an underwater studio.

00:56:12.640 --> 00:56:17.040
Using a range of waterproof lights and time lapse cameras, they hope to capture the growth

00:56:17.040 --> 00:56:28.400
of bizarre underwater ice formations.

00:56:28.400 --> 00:56:33.440
Over the coming weeks, the dive team would go below the ice over 100 times to film the

00:56:33.440 --> 00:56:39.880
extraordinary secret world on the lower flanks of Mount Erebus.

00:56:39.880 --> 00:56:43.520
On top of the ice, the orca team has repositioned.

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:47.840
Their new goal is to get underwater shots of the whales.

00:56:47.840 --> 00:56:50.560
They don't dare to get in the water with orcas.

00:56:50.560 --> 00:56:56.960
Attaching a camera to a pole is a safer option, provided the whales aren't put off by it.

00:56:56.960 --> 00:57:11.240
No one is prepared for what happens next.

00:57:11.240 --> 00:57:32.520
The entire pod arrives.

00:57:32.520 --> 00:57:38.440
Eyeball to eyeball, this is about as close to killer whales as it's possible to get.

00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:43.480
By using multiple crews and cameras, the frozen planet team have been able to capture the

00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:49.600
full Erebus story, from the fire at its crater down to the whales that patrol its frozen

00:57:49.600 --> 00:57:50.600
shores.

00:57:50.600 --> 00:58:10.920
It's quite a privilege to feel a whale breath on your face.

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:37.220
And yes, just laugh.
